# Waitlist — interest capture as a workbook

A worked example of **data capture as a `.work` extension**: a "notify me" list, written as a
`resource` (the data model) plus a `server` block (the validation + write), and served by the nexus
like any workbook. The runtime ships none of this — capturing signups is our concern, so it lives in
our own workbook, on the platform's generic primitives (`Nexus.Store`, `Nexus.Live`).

## The data model

`resource` is a typed, persisted declaration — rows live in the nexus's managed store
(`Nexus.Store`), the same backend every workbook gets. One row per signup.

resource Signup do
  email :text
  interest :text
  at :int
end

## The capture

`register/0` exposes a live source the lander calls on submit. The handler validates the email shape,
normalizes it, and persists a `Signup` — plain Elixir in a `.work` file. A maintainer reads the list
with `Nexus.Store.all(Signup)` (an auth-gated surface, not exposed here).

server :waitlist do
  def register do
    Nexus.Live.register("waitlist", fn params, emit ->
      emit.(add(params["email"], params["interest"] || ""))
    end)
  end

  @doc "Validate + persist one signup. Returns %{ok: true} | %{ok: false, error: …}."
  def add(email, interest) when is_binary(email) do
    email = email |> String.trim() |> String.downcase()
    interest = interest |> to_string() |> String.slice(0, 80)

    if valid_email?(email) do
      Nexus.Store.create(Signup, %{email: email, interest: interest, at: System.system_time(:second)})
      %{ok: true}
    else
      %{ok: false, error: "that doesn't look like an email"}
    end
  end

  def add(_, _), do: %{ok: false, error: "email required"}

  @doc "All signups, newest first (maintainer read)."
  def list, do: Nexus.Store.all(Signup) |> Enum.sort_by(& &1.at, :desc)

  # Deliberately-loose RFC-ish check: one @, a dot in the domain, no spaces.
  defp valid_email?(e), do: Regex.match?(~r/^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/, e)
end
